Task 1: Build a Secure Helm Chart with Secrets and Security Contexts
In this task you will scaffold a Helm chart and build all of its templates and values in the correct order so that helm install succeeds on the first try. Unlike a real-world iterative workflow, here you will write the complete values.yaml file up front (Subtask 1.1) before writing any template that references it. This avoids the common mistake of referencing values fields that do not exist yet.

Subtask 1.1: Scaffold the Chart and Define the Complete values.yaml
# Create a new Helm chart
helm create secure-app
cd secure-app

# Remove default templates we will not use, keep the structure
rm -f templates/tests/test-connection.yaml
rm -f templates/hpa.yaml templates/ingress.yaml

# Overwrite values.yaml completely with every field the templates in this
# lab will need. Writing this file first means every template we create
# afterward can safely reference these fields.
cat <<'EOF' > values.yaml
replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: nginx
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
  tag: "alpine"

imagePullSecrets: []
nameOverride: ""
fullnameOverride: ""

podAnnotations: {}

podSecurityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  runAsGroup: 3000
  fsGroup: 2000
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000
  capabilities:
    drop:
    - ALL
    add:
    - NET_BIND_SERVICE

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80

resources:
  limits:
    cpu: 500m
    memory: 512Mi
  requests:
    cpu: 250m
    memory: 256Mi

autoscaling:
  enabled: false

nodeSelector: {}
tolerations: []
affinity: {}

serviceAccount:
  create: true
  name: ""

rbac:
  create: true

networkPolicy:
  enabled: true

resourceQuota:
  enabled: true
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "1"
    requests.memory: 1Gi
    limits.cpu: "2"
    limits.memory: 2Gi
    persistentvolumeclaims: "4"
    pods: "10"

monitoring:
  enabled: true

# Application secrets (for lab demonstration only; never commit real
# secrets like this to source control in a production project)
secrets:
  database-password: "supersecretpassword"
  api-key: "my-secret-api-key"
  jwt-secret: "jwt-signing-secret"
EOF
Deliverable: A values.yaml file containing every key listed above. Verify it parses correctly:

helm lint . || true
cat values.yaml | grep -c "^[a-zA-Z]"
Expected result: no YAML parsing errors are reported (lint will warn about missing templates referencing icons, which is fine at this stage since templates are not yet created).

Subtask 1.2: Create the Secret Template
cat <<'EOF' > templates/secret.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-secret
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
type: Opaque
data:
  {{- range $key, $value := .Values.secrets }}
  {{ $key }}: {{ $value | b64enc | quote }}
  {{- end }}
EOF
Deliverable: Render the template standalone to confirm it is syntactically valid before wiring it into a full deployment:

helm template secure-app-release . --show-only templates/secret.yaml
Expected result: valid YAML output showing a Secret object with three base64-encoded data keys (database-password, api-key, jwt-secret).

Subtask 1.3: Create the Deployment Template Using the Secret
Because values.yaml already defines podSecurityContext, securityContext, autoscaling, and replicaCount, this deployment template will render successfully.

cat <<'EOF' > templates/deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  {{- if not .Values.autoscaling.enabled }}
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
  {{- end }}
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      {{- include "secure-app.selectorLabels" . | nindent 6 }}
  template:
    metadata:
      {{- with .Values.podAnnotations }}
      annotations:
        {{- toYaml . | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}
      labels:
        {{- include "secure-app.selectorLabels" . | nindent 8 }}
    spec:
      {{- with .Values.imagePullSecrets }}
      imagePullSecrets:
        {{- toYaml . | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}
      serviceAccountName: {{ include "secure-app.serviceAccountName" . }}
      securityContext:
        {{- toYaml .Values.podSecurityContext | nindent 8 }}
      containers:
      - name: {{ .Chart.Name }}
        securityContext:
          {{- toYaml .Values.securityContext | nindent 12 }}
        image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}"
        imagePullPolicy: {{ .Values.image.pullPolicy }}
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-secret
              key: database-password
        - name: API_KEY
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-secret
              key: api-key
        - name: JWT_SECRET
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-secret
              key: jwt-secret
        ports:
        - name: http
          containerPort: 80
          protocol: TCP
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /
            port: http
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /
            port: http
        resources:
          {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }}
      {{- with .Values.nodeSelector }}
      nodeSelector:
        {{- toYaml . | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}
      {{- with .Values.affinity }}
      affinity:
        {{- toYaml . | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}
      {{- with .Values.tolerations }}
      tolerations:
        {{- toYaml . | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}
EOF
Also add the ServiceAccount name helper, needed by serviceAccountName above:

cat <<'EOF' >> templates/_helpers.tpl

{{/*
Create the name of the service account to use
*/}}
{{- define "secure-app.serviceAccountName" -}}
{{- if .Values.serviceAccount.create }}
{{- default (printf "%s-sa" (include "secure-app.fullname" .)) .Values.serviceAccount.name }}
{{- else }}
{{- default "default" .Values.serviceAccount.name }}
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
EOF
Deliverable: Run a full dry-run render of the chart and confirm there are no template errors:

helm template secure-app-release . --namespace secure-apps
helm lint .
Expected result: helm lint . reports 0 chart(s) failed, and helm template prints a complete Deployment manifest with image: "nginx:alpine", replicas: 2, and the env block referencing secure-app-release-secure-app-secret.

Task 2: Deploy and Secure the Chart with RBAC and Network Policy
Subtask 2.1: Add RBAC Template
Create a dedicated ServiceAccount, Role, and RoleBinding so the workload does not use the default service account.

cat <<'EOF' > templates/rbac.yaml
{{- if .Values.rbac.create }}
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.serviceAccountName" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-role
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "endpoints"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-rolebinding
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: {{ include "secure-app.serviceAccountName" . }}
  namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
{{- end }}
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Add a Network Policy
cat <<'EOF' > templates/networkpolicy.yaml
{{- if .Values.networkPolicy.enabled }}
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-netpol
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      {{- include "secure-app.selectorLabels" . | nindent 6 }}
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
  egress:
  - to: []
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
  - to:
    - namespaceSelector: {}
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 443
{{- end }}
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Deploy the Chart
# Install the chart
helm install secure-app-release . --namespace secure-apps --create-namespace

# Verify the secret was created
kubectl get secrets -n secure-apps
kubectl describe secret secure-app-release-secure-app-secret -n secure-apps

# Check the deployment is using the secrets
kubectl get deployment -n secure-apps -o yaml | grep -A 15 "env:"

# Verify pods are running
kubectl get pods -n secure-apps
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=secure-app-release -n secure-apps --timeout=90s

# Verify RBAC and network policy objects exist
kubectl get serviceaccounts,roles,rolebindings -n secure-apps
kubectl get networkpolicies -n secure-apps
Deliverable: kubectl get pods -n secure-apps shows all pods in Running state with READY 1/1. kubectl get secrets -n secure-apps lists secure-app-release-secure-app-secret of type Opaque. kubectl get networkpolicies -n secure-apps lists secure-app-release-secure-app-netpol.

Task 3: Validate Security with Helm Tests and a Compliance Script
Subtask 3.1: Add a Helm Chart Test
The test pod below must be able to demonstrate a read-only root filesystem while still confirming the secret env var is set. To do this correctly, the container security context uses readOnlyRootFilesystem: true on the container, but the test only attempts to write to /tmp, which is backed by an emptyDir volume mounted specifically to allow the write-permission check to run without contradicting the read-only root filesystem requirement.

mkdir -p templates/tests

cat <<'EOF' > templates/tests/security-test.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: "{{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-security-test"
  labels:
    {{- include "secure-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": test
spec:
  restartPolicy: Never
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
    runAsGroup: 3000
    fsGroup: 2000
  containers:
  - name: security-test
    image: alpine:latest
    command:
    - /bin/sh
    - -c
    - |
      echo "Running security tests..."

      # Test 1: Check if secrets are properly mounted as env vars
      if [ -n "$DATABASE_PASSWORD" ]; then
        echo "PASS: Database password environment variable is set"
      else
        echo "FAIL: Database password environment variable is missing"
        exit 1
      fi

      # Test 2: Verify non-root user
      if [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "PASS: Running as non-root user (uid=$(id -u))"
      else
        echo "FAIL: Running as root user"
        exit 1
      fi

      # Test 3: Confirm root filesystem is read-only, using the writable
      # scratch volume mounted at /scratch for the write-permission check
      if ! touch /root-write-test 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "PASS: Root filesystem is read-only"
      else
        echo "FAIL: Root filesystem is writable"
        rm -f /root-write-test
        exit 1
      fi

      # Test 4: Confirm the writable scratch volume works as expected
      if touch /scratch/scratch-write-test 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "PASS: Scratch volume is writable as expected"
        rm -f /scratch/scratch-write-test
      else
        echo "FAIL: Scratch volume should be writable but is not"
        exit 1
      fi

      echo "All security tests passed!"
    env:
    - name: DATABASE_PASSWORD
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: {{ include "secure-app.fullname" . }}-secret
          key: database-password
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      runAsNonRoot: true
      runAsUser: 1000
      capabilities:
        drop:
        - ALL
    volumeMounts:
    - name: scratch
      mountPath: /scratch
  volumes:
  - name: scratch
    emptyDir: {}
EOF
Upgrade the release so the test hook is registered, then run the test:

helm upgrade secure-app-release . --namespace secure-apps
helm test secure-app-release --namespace secure-apps --logs
Deliverable: Output includes PASS: Database password environment variable is set, PASS: Running as non-root user (uid=1000), PASS: Root filesystem is read-only, PASS: Scratch volume is writable as expected, and All security tests passed!. The overall helm test command exits with the message TEST SUITE: ... secure-app-release-secure-app-security-test and phase Succeeded.

Subtask 3.2: Run a Security Validation Script
cat <<'EOF' > validate-security.sh
#!/bin/bash
set -e

echo "Running Helm security validation..."

echo "1. Checking for hardcoded secrets outside of secretKeyRef..."
if grep -rE "password|secret|key" templates/ --include="*.yaml" | grep -vE "secretKeyRef|valueFrom|secure-app.labels|secure-app.selectorLabels|secure-app.fullname"; then
    echo "WARNING: Potential hardcoded secrets found!"
else
    echo "PASS: No hardcoded secrets detected in templates"
fi

echo "2. Checking for privileged containers..."
if grep -r "privileged: true" templates/ --include="*.yaml"; then
    echo "WARNING: Privileged containers found!"
else
    echo "PASS: No privileged containers detected"
fi

echo "3. Checking for root user usage..."
if grep -r "runAsUser: 0" templates/ --include="*.yaml"; then
    echo "WARNING: Containers running as root found!"
else
    echo "PASS: No containers running as root"
fi

echo "4. Checking for security contexts..."
if grep -rl "securityContext" templates/ --include="*.yaml" > /dev/null; then
    echo "PASS: Security contexts found"
else
    echo "WARNING: No security contexts defined!"
fi

echo "Security validation complete."
EOF

chmod +x validate-security.sh
./validate-security.sh
Deliverable: All four checks print PASS. If any prints WARNING, review the corresponding template file and correct it before proceeding.

Subtask 3.3: Run a Compliance Check Against the Live Cluster
cat <<'EOF' > compliance-check.sh
#!/bin/bash

NAMESPACE="secure-apps"
RELEASE="secure-app-release"

echo "=== Helm Security Compliance Check ==="
echo "Checking compliance for release: $RELEASE in namespace: $NAMESPACE"
echo

echo "1. Checking for non-root user execution..."
if kubectl get pods -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=$RELEASE -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.securityContext.runAsUser}' | grep -q "1000"; then
    echo "   PASS: Containers running as non-root user"
else
    echo "   FAIL: Containers may be running as root"
fi

echo "2. Checking for read-only root filesystem..."
if kubectl get pods -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=$RELEASE -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem}' | grep -q "true"; then
    echo "   PASS: Read-only root filesystem enabled"
else
    echo "   FAIL: Root filesystem is writable"
fi

echo "3. Checking for privilege escalation prevention..."
if kubectl get pods -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=$RELEASE -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].securityContext.allowPrivilegeEscalation}' | grep -q "false"; then
    echo "   PASS: Privilege escalation disabled"
else
    echo "   FAIL: Privilege escalation may be allowed"
fi

echo "4. Checking secrets management..."
if kubectl get secrets -n "$NAMESPACE" | grep -q "$RELEASE"; then
    echo "   PASS: Secrets properly managed by Helm"
else
    echo "   FAIL: No Helm-managed secrets found"
fi

echo "5. Checking network policies..."
if kubectl get networkpolicies -n "$NAMESPACE" | grep -q "$RELEASE"; then
    echo "   PASS: Network policies configured"
else
    echo "   WARNING: No network policies found"
fi

echo "6. Checking resource limits..."
if kubectl get pods -n "$NAMESPACE" -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=$RELEASE -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].resources.limits}' | grep -qE "cpu|memory"; then
    echo "   PASS: Resource limits configured"
else
    echo "   FAIL: No resource limits found"
fi

echo "7. Checking RBAC configuration..."
if kubectl get serviceaccounts,roles,rolebindings -n "$NAMESPACE" | grep -q "$RELEASE"; then
    echo "   PASS: RBAC properly configured"
else
    echo "   FAIL: RBAC not configured"
fi

echo
echo "=== Compliance Check Complete ==="
EOF

chmod +x compliance-check.sh
./compliance-check.sh
Deliverable: All seven checks report PASS (network policy check may report WARNING only if you disabled networkPolicy.enabled earlier, which you did not). A fully passing run confirms the chart built in Task 1 and deployed in Task 2 meets the security baseline.

