Subtask 4.2: Document Security Best Practices
cat <<'EOF' > helm-security-best-practices.md
# Helm Security Best Practices

## 1. Secret Management
- Never hardcode secrets in values.yaml or templates committed to source control
- Use Kubernetes Secrets with proper RBAC restricting who can read them
- Consider external secret management systems (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault)
- Encrypt sensitive values files using the helm-secrets plugin or SOPS
- Rotate secrets regularly and avoid reusing the same secret across environments

## 2. Container Security
- Always run containers as non-root users (runAsNonRoot: true)
- Use read-only root filesystems and mount writable emptyDir volumes only where needed
- Disable privilege escalation (allowPrivilegeEscalation: false)
- Drop all unnecessary Linux capabilities and add back only what is required
- Set seccomp profiles to RuntimeDefault or a custom restricted profile

## 3. RBAC and Access Control
- Create dedicated service accounts for each application instead of using default
- Follow the principle of least privilege when defining Role rules
- Use Role and RoleBinding scoped to a namespace instead of ClusterRole when possible
- Regularly audit RBAC permissions with kubectl auth can-i and automated tooling

## 4. Network Security
- Implement NetworkPolicy resources to restrict pod ingress and egress traffic
- Default-deny all traffic and explicitly allow only required ports and namespaces
- Use a service mesh (such as Istio or Linkerd) for mutual TLS and advanced traffic policies
- Monitor ingress and egress traffic patterns to detect anomalies
- Restrict egress to DNS and required external endpoints only

## 5. Image Security
- Pin image tags to specific versions or digests rather than using "latest"
- Scan images for vulnerabilities using tools such as Trivy or Grype before deployment
- Use minimal base images (distroless or alpine) to reduce attack surface
- Verify image provenance with signing tools such as Cosign
- Restrict imagePullPolicy to Always in production to avoid stale cached images

## 6. Helm Repository Management
- Only add Helm repositories from trusted, verified sources
- Pin chart versions explicitly instead of always pulling the latest version
- Review chart templates before installing charts from third-party repositories
- Use `helm repo update` regularly but validate changelogs before upgrading
- Maintain an internal chart repository for organization-approved charts

## 7. Supply Chain Security
- Sign Helm chart packages using `helm package --sign` and verify signatures on install
- Use provenance files (.prov) to confirm chart integrity
- Integrate chart linting (`helm lint`) and security scanning into CI/CD pipelines
- Track chart dependencies and audit subchart sources for vulnerabilities
- Enforce policy-as-code checks (such as OPA/Gatekeeper or Kyverno) before deployment

## 8. Incident Response
- Maintain audit logs of all `helm install`, `upgrade`, and `rollback` operations
- Define a rollback plan using `helm rollback` for quickly reverting insecure releases
- Establish alerting for privileged containers, root user execution, or missing security contexts
- Document a runbook for revoking compromised secrets and rotating credentials quickly
- Practice incident response drills that include Helm release rollback and secret rotation
EOF

cat helm-security-best-practices.md
Deliverable: A file helm-security-best-practices.md containing all eight numbered sections, each with complete bullet points and no truncated lines. Running cat helm-security-best-practices.md displays the full document from # Helm Security Best Practices through the end of the Incident Response section.

Expected Outcomes
A working Helm chart (secure-app) whose templates reference only values that are defined in values.yaml from the start, installing cleanly with helm install on the first attempt
A deployed release in the secure-apps namespace with verified non-root security contexts, RBAC, network policies, and secret-backed environment variables
A passing helm test suite and a compliance script that reports PASS on all seven security checks, plus a completed best-practices reference document
Conclusion
In this lab you built a complete, security-hardened Helm chart from the ground up, deliberately writing the full values.yaml before any template so that every helm install and helm template invocation succeeded without missing-field errors. You layered in Kubernetes Secrets, pod and container security contexts, RBAC, network policies, and a Helm chart test that correctly reconciles a read-only root filesystem with a writable scratch volume, then validated the whole deployment with both a static template scanner and a live cluster compliance script. Finally, you produced a complete best-practices reference document covering secret management, container hardening, RBAC, network security, image security, repository management, supply chain integrity, and incident response. These skills translate directly to real-world Helm usage: writing values schemas defensively, validating templates before deployment, and continuously auditing running releases are exactly the habits that prevent security misconfigurations from reaching production Kubernetes clusters.
